./experiments/2026-06-03/EXP-0017/committee1/systems_reviewer.out:12:RATIONALE: No shipping OSS system found that directly implements “skip retrieval because the needed content is already in the current live context window.” Existing systems already solve adjacent redundancy at the inference layer via token-prefix/KV reuse: SGLang explicitly retains KV for prompts and generation results in a radix tree for prefix search/reuse/eviction, while vLLM and TensorRT-LLM reuse hashed/paged KV blocks for same-prefix requests. That makes this not an inference-systems cache insight; it is a RAG-layer retrieval-suppression mechanism plus measurement protocol. The packet’s evidence supports the mechanism on a constructed generator: redundant fraction 0.235-0.816, lexical AUC 0.835-1.000, and matched-random accuracy delta CI > 0 with tau=0.3 skipping 39% at modeled accuracy 1.000. However, because the workload, redundancy labels, and task-success model are generated, the systems claim does not yet hold at realistic scale/workload. L1 must use real agent+RAG logs with in-context-presence labels, production-like retrieval costs, a real sentence embedding/check, and end-to-end model accuracy.
